defects.evidence:
Bash, measured: `x=$(echo hi
)#y
echo "[$x]"` is `bash -n` clean and prints `[hi#y]` - the `#` is inside the word. At HEAD: `code('x=$(echo hi
)#y
echo "[$x]"')` returns `'x=$(echo hi
)
echo "[$x]"'` (the `#y` deleted), while `_shell_scan` on the same input keeps `)#y` intact. The same divergence on the LOUD-convention script `x=$(echo hi
)#; echo LOUD
echo DONE`: bash=not-a-comment, `_shell_scan`=not-a-comment, `_uncomment`=comment. The shipped step ships this construct - .github/workflows/account-skill-zips.yml:262-289 is `verdict=$(python3 - <<'PY' ... PY
) || verdict=""`. Two added claims are false as a result: scripts/test_account_workflows.py:403-405 "every scanner in this file asks `_WordParens` and `_COMMENT_OPENS_AFTER` the same question - differing only in where quote state is tracked", and the docstring of test_this_files_scanners_agree_with_bash_about_where_a_comment_opens (:1085) which names this drift as the thing it holds. It cannot hold it: it slices `line = next(l for l in script.splitlines() if "LOUD" in l)` and every script in `_COMMENT_OPENER_SCRIPTS` keeps its `#` on one line, so no entry can express a multi-line substitution. `_uncomment`'s own "WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT MODEL" paragraph concedes per-LINE quoting and says nothing about per-line paren state.

`_WordParens.saw` pushes one flag per unquoted `(` and pops on every unquoted `)`. A `case` pattern terminator has no matching `(` (its `(` is optional and normally omitted - the docstring says so and relies on it), so it consumes the `$(`'s True flag and the real closing `)` then sees an empty stack and is classified as an operator. Measured, each `bash -n` clean and each reaching DONE: `x=$(case y in y) echo hi ;; esac)#; echo LOUD` -> bash does NOT open a comment (prints LOUD), `_shell_scan` and `_uncomment` both DO. Same for the multi-line form `x=$(
case y in
  y) echo hi ;;
esac
)#; echo LOUD`, for a two-arm variant, and for `x=$(for i in 1; do case $i in 1) echo hi;; esac; done)#; echo LOUD`. This is code deleted from the scan, which the class docstring at :70-72 calls out as the costlier failure - "Treating every `)` as an operator invents a comment where bash sees data, which deletes code from the scan" - while presenting the stack model as deciding it correctly. `_WordParens` has no limitations section, `_COMMENT_OPENER_SCRIPTS` has no nested-`case` entry, and `test_every_opener_this_file_models_is_executed_by_the_table` only requires both ANSWERS of the `)` rule to appear, not both mechanisms.
